---
/**
 * Privacy policy.
 *
 * Every factual claim on this page was checked against the source before it was written:
 *  - `app/Sidewire/Resources/Info.plist` — `SUEnableAutomaticChecks` is `<false/>`, so Sparkle's
 *    appcast fetch is opt-in and off until the user turns it on in Settings.
 *  - The app's own Swift sources contain no `URLSession`, no HTTP client, and no analytics or
 *    crash-reporting SDK of any kind. Sparkle is the only network-facing dependency.
 * Do not add a claim here that you have not checked the same way.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { site, links } from '../data/site';

// Update by hand whenever the text below changes.
const updated = '5 August 2026';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="Sidewire collects nothing. No accounts, no analytics, no telemetry — and the app's only network call is an opt-in update check."
>
  <Nav />
  <main id="main" class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">Legal</span>
      <h1>Privacy</h1>
      <p class="prose-meta">Last updated {updated}</p>

      <p>
        The short version: {site.name} collects nothing about you, because it has nowhere to put it.
        There are no accounts, no servers of ours in the path, no analytics and no telemetry. What
        follows is the long version, so you can check the claim rather than take it.
      </p>

      <h2>The app</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} makes exactly one network call that is not the connection between your own two
        machines: it asks a signed update feed whether a newer version exists.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          That update check is <strong>opt‑in and off by default</strong> — the app never reaches
          the internet on its own until you turn on “Automatically check for updates”, or press the
          check button yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          The request is an ordinary HTTPS fetch of a small XML file hosted alongside the project's
          releases. Whoever hosts the file sees what any web server sees for a download: an IP
          address, a timestamp and a user agent. It carries no identifier we created.
        </li>
        <li>
          Updates are verified with an EdDSA signature before they are installed. An update that
          does not verify is refused.
        </li>
        <li>
          There is no other HTTP anywhere in the app: no analytics SDK, no crash reporter, no
          licence check, no “anonymous usage statistics”.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Your screen</h2>
      <p>
        The video of your second desktop and the keyboard and mouse events that go back the other
        way travel <strong>directly between your two machines</strong>, over your cable or your own
        network, inside a TLS 1.3 connection. None of it is routed through a service, stored, or
        seen by anyone else. There is no cloud component to this product.
      </p>

      <h2>What stays on your machines</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A device identity keypair and the record of which machines you have paired with, so a
          paired reconnect can skip the PIN. Both are kept locally.
        </li>
        <li>Your app settings.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Nothing above is transmitted anywhere. Deleting the app and its preferences removes it.
        “Forget this Mac” drops a specific pairing.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        This page and the rest of the site are static files. There is no JavaScript, no cookie, no
        web font, no CDN, no embedded video, no tracking pixel and no third‑party request of any
        kind — everything you load comes from this one origin. Nothing here identifies you and
        nothing is stored in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        The web server that serves these files keeps the ordinary access log every web server keeps
        (IP address, time, requested path, user agent, referrer) for operating and debugging the
        site. That is the only record the site produces.
      </p>

      <h2>Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        We do not sell, share, rent or transfer personal data, because we do not collect any.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} is a utility for connecting two computers you own and is not directed at
        children. As above, it collects no personal data from anyone.
      </p>

      <h2>Questions and changes</h2>
      <p>
        This policy will change if the product does — for instance if a release ever introduces a
        network feature this page does not describe. The date at the top is the date of the last
        change, and the full history of this file is public in the
        <a href={links.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener">repository</a>. Questions and
        corrections are best raised as an issue there.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
